# JWlink2

JWlink2 is an independent linker for Windows COFF objects and Microsoft `.lib` archives. Its primary target is AMD64 PE32+ executables and DLLs; an explicitly selected, limited x86 PE32 executable path is also available. The existing JWlink remains unchanged and is still the full-featured linker.

## Build

Build the `JWlink2` project in `source\Tools\Tools.sln` for either Win32 or x64. The generated linker executable is written to the repository's `bin\tools\<Configuration>\<Platform>` directory.

## Usage

```text
JWlink2 /OUT:app.exe /ENTRY:main /SUBSYSTEM:CONSOLE app.obj helpers.lib
JWlink2 /OUT:gui.exe /ENTRY:guiStartup /SUBSYSTEM:WINDOWS gui.obj user32.lib kernel32.lib
JWlink2 /MACHINE:X86 /ENTRY:_main /OUT:app32.exe app32.obj helpers32.lib
JWlink2 /DLL /DEF:exports.def /OUT:plugin.dll plugin.obj
JWlink2 @link.rsp
```

In AMD64 switch mode, a console executable defaults to `mainCRTStartup`, falling back to `main`. For a Windows-subsystem executable, an already defined `WinMainCRTStartup` takes priority; otherwise an already defined `mainCRTStartup` or `main` retains the existing fallback. If none is defined, JWlink2 searches loaded archives for `WinMainCRTStartup`, falling back to an archive's `mainCRTStartup` if no GUI startup is available. Use `/ENTRY:<symbol>` to override selection, including to force extraction of a startup from a library or to select `wWinMainCRTStartup`. For DLLs, JWlink2 selects `DllMainCRTStartup` when present; otherwise the DLL entry RVA is zero, and `/NOENTRY` explicitly omits it. In JWlink directive mode, `option start=<symbol>` or a COFF `/ENTRY` directive selects the entry. Otherwise, an EXE warns and uses the first output section, approximating JWlink's first-group fallback; a DLL has a zero entry RVA. Supply an explicit start for predictable startup, especially with multiple sections. The selected entry is called directly by the Windows loader; JWlink2 does not provide or initialize the C runtime, infer a `WinMain`/`wWinMain` wrapper, or supply CRT libraries. The default subsystem is console; use `/SUBSYSTEM:WINDOWS`, `system nt_win`, or `runtime windows` for a GUI image.

Switch mode defaults to `a.exe` or `a.dll`; JWlink directive mode uses the first input's stem in the current directory unless `name` is given. Libraries specified by the command line or COFF `/DEFAULTLIB` are searched as needed; `/NODEFAULTLIB` can exclude default libraries but not explicit archive inputs. No CRT library is implicitly added. Unresolved-symbol diagnostics list known referencing objects; duplicate-definition errors identify both objects. JWlink2 returns 0 on success and 1 on diagnosed errors.

Supported options include `/OUT`, `/DLL`, `/DEF`, `/EXPORT`, `/IMPLIB`, `/ENTRY`, `/NOENTRY`, `/SUBSYSTEM` (including a major.minor version), `/MACHINE:X64` and bounded `/MACHINE:X86`, `/LIBPATH`, `/DEFAULTLIB`, `/NODEFAULTLIB`, `/DELAYLOAD:<dll>`, `/ALIGN`, `/BASE`, `/FIXED`, `/DYNAMICBASE`, `/NXCOMPAT`, `/MAP`, `/INCREMENTAL:NO`, and `@response-file`. DLLs with exports generate an import library beside the output DLL by default; `/IMPLIB:<file>` selects another path. `.def` files support `LIBRARY`/`NAME` and `EXPORTS` entries with aliases, ordinals, `NONAME`, `DATA`, and `PRIVATE`. COFF `.drectve` sections recognize `/DEFAULTLIB`, `/NODEFAULTLIB`, `/DELAYLOAD`, `/ALIGN`, `/ALIGNCOMM`, `/ENTRY`, `/EXPORT`, and `/INCLUDE`; exports produce a PE export directory, and forced includes participate in archive extraction. GNU-style `-o`, `-L`, and `-l` forms are also accepted. Malformed or unsupported directives fail explicitly. Other options with unsupported behavior also fail explicitly.

## Current scope

JWlink2 defaults to native Windows AMD64 PE32+ executables and DLLs. `/MACHINE:X86` selects a limited I386 PE32 executable target regardless of the linker host architecture. It requires an explicit `/ENTRY` using the exact COFF symbol spelling (for example `_main`), and accepts matching x86 COFF objects and Microsoft archives, including short import objects. It supports I386 `ABSOLUTE`, `DIR32`, `DIR32NB`, `REL32`, `SECTION` and `SECREL` relocations, 32-bit import tables and function thunks, and HIGHLOW base relocations. The default x86 image base is `0x400000`. Tests execute synthetic and MSVC-produced objects/static libraries, compare with Microsoft LINK and dumpbin, and execute SDK `kernel32.lib` imports through both direct IAT calls and function thunks on Windows. The x86 tools/SDK tests log a skip when unavailable.

This x86 path is not a general 32-bit JWlink replacement: x86 DLL/export generation, delay loading, PDB output, embedded manifests, input resource/TLS/exception/load-configuration metadata, CRT startup and unlisted relocations are unsupported; unsupported options and metadata are rejected. Ordinal/data imports, actual rebasing, GUI operation, BigObj and broader x86 toolchain compatibility remain unvalidated. Only switch mode selects x86; JWlink directive mode remains AMD64. JWlink2 does not produce DOS, OS/2, Win16, VxD, Phar Lap, Novell, QNX, ELF, or raw images. See [MISSING_FEATURES.md](MISSING_FEATURES.md) for the tested boundary and repository compatibility checklist.

The GUI integration test compiles a minimal CRT-free AMD64 startup with MSVC, links against Windows SDK `user32.lib` and `kernel32.lib` with JWlink2 (switch mode, `system nt_win`, and `runtime windows`) and Microsoft LINK, and executes each image to create, verify, and destroy a hidden window. It checks GUI PE headers and imports with dumpbin. This requires MSVC, the SDK and a usable Windows window station; it does not establish full CRT, WinMain, event-loop or application-manifest compatibility.

COFF input sections are grouped by their name before `$` and sorted by the full, case-sensitive name within each group. Equal names retain input-object and section order, including selected archive members. Alignment, relocations and selected associative COMDAT mappings follow the sorted contributions. When `/MERGE` combines different section families, those families retain first-seen order and each family is sorted separately. Runtime tests execute reversed `.tls$*` callbacks and manually invoked `.CRT$X*` constructor pointers; they do not supply CRT startup. In a separate fixture JWlink also orders A before Z but groups them in `.rdata` with different padding.

Ordinary COFF and BigObj relocation-overflow tables are supported, including archive members. The overflow flag requires a 0xffff header count and a valid first record whose count includes that record; malformed or truncated tables are rejected. The object-only overflow flag is not copied into PE section flags. Runtime AMD64 relocations support `ABSOLUTE`, `ADDR64`, `ADDR32`, `ADDR32NB`, `REL32` through `REL32_5`, `SECTION`, and `SECREL`. `SECTION` and `SECREL` use final output-section coordinates; an absolute `SECTION` target uses the index after the last output section, while an absolute `SECREL` target is rejected. `SECREL7`, managed `TOKEN`, `SREL32`, `PAIR`, and `SSPAN32` are not supported. Separately, `/DEBUG` consumes CodeView `.debug$S` `SECTION` and `SECREL` relocations in object coordinates and rejects other types; `.debug$T` relocations are unsupported. MSVC `/O1 /Z7` TLS code and MASM overflow objects are checked when the x64 tools are installed; the TLS fixture verifies linked bytes rather than TLS startup. Boundary and malformed tables are always covered by synthetic tests.

JWlink2 generates PE import descriptors, lookup tables, IAT entries, ordinary function thunks, and delay-import descriptors. `/DELAYLOAD:<dll>` and COFF `.drectve` delay-load directives currently support one function import in the link; requests with additional delay-loaded functions, data imports, or DLLs without selected imports are rejected explicitly. The delay IAT entry initially points to a generated AMD64 resolver which calls eager `KERNEL32.dll` imports of `LoadLibraryA` and `GetProcAddress`, preserves the Microsoft x64 argument registers, caches the resolved function address in the delay IAT, and tail-jumps to it. A Windows runtime test checks that the provider is initially unloaded, that the first call writes the actual function address into the IAT, and that a second call uses the cached address. Missing DLLs or symbols raise noncontinuable delay-load exceptions (`0xC06D007E`/`0xC06D007F`); resolver unwind records are emitted. Delay module handles, bound/unload IATs, delayimp hooks, and unload semantics are not implemented; the resolved function retains its `LoadLibraryA` reference for the image lifetime. Concurrent first calls can each acquire a DLL reference. Ordinary imports retain their existing eager-IAT path.

JWlink2 reads Microsoft COFF archives, including NUL-terminated Microsoft and slash-newline long names, and short AMD64 import objects. Its generated import libraries contain COFF descriptor, null-descriptor and null-thunk members as well as short import objects. Tests execute named, ordinal-only and data imports through MSVC-produced and JWlink2-produced libraries in both JWlink2 and MSVC LINK, including long DLL names and two generated libraries in one MSVC link. A JWlib `-fac -i6 -ic` import and the installed Windows SDK `kernel32.lib` are also linked and executed by JWlink2. Real MSVC and JWlib COFF static archives exercise long names, selective extraction and explicit-library order versus repeated defaults. MSVC and SDK tests log a skip when their tools are absent. These fixtures do not establish compatibility with every JWlib archive variant or JWlink's selection behavior; see the checklist for the JWlink crash encountered in the comparison.

`/DEBUG` writes a PE RSDS debug directory entry and an MSF 7.0 PDB beside the output (`<output>.pdb`); `/PDB:<file>` selects another path, and the RSDS GUID/age match the PDB. The linker consumes MSVC `/Z7` CodeView object data (`.debug$T` type records including field lists, method lists, string/build-info ids, and `.debug$S` symbols, file checksums, string tables, and line subsections), remaps it to final RVAs, and writes the PDB info, TPI, DBI (modules, section contributions, section map, file info), empty IPI, `/names`, GSI, PSI, symbol-record, and section-header streams plus per-module symbol/line streams. Output images are validated against PE layout invariants and the Windows loader (including rebasing of DLLs with DIR64 relocations) in `JWlink2Test`. COMDAT sections without an external symbol (MSVC `/Gy` static functions) are kept unmerged. Windows `dbghelp` resolves public symbols, source files, and lines from the result (covered by `JWlink2Test`). Unsupported CodeView leaves and subsections (inline lines, frame data, cross-scope records) are rejected explicitly; separate IPI/TPI hash streams are not produced and LLVM `llvm-pdbutil` validation has not been performed.

This PDB workflow is a JWlink2 extension, not JWlink debug-artifact compatibility. In a JWasm `-win64 -Zi` fixture, JWlink `debug codeview` writes embedded NB05 CodeView with separate source-line, local-symbol and type subsections. `debug codeview lines` omits type and local subsections, while `debug codeview types,locals` omits source lines. JWlink2 rejects these selective modifiers and the fixture's legacy signature-1 CodeView objects when debug output is requested; the object can be linked without debug output. JWlink `debug dwarf`, `debug watcom` and `debug all` with no previously selected format do not produce a PE CodeView debug directory in this fixture. JWlink2's existing `debug all` instead enables its RSDS/PDB extension; DWARF and Watcom output are not implemented. Tests parse JWlink NB05 public/source/line records and compare an actual MSVC `/Z7` object's `main` symbol and source line through Windows `dbghelp` for JWlink2 and Microsoft LINK PDBs. The MSVC comparison logs a skip when the compiler is unavailable; no equivalent debugger-consumer validation of JWlink's DWARF, Watcom or NB05 output is claimed.

### Map files

`/MAP[:file]` and `option map[=file]` produce the same JWlink2-specific text format. It lists output sections (absolute virtual address, virtual size, name), defined global symbols ordered by address then name, and selected input contributions (absolute virtual address, size, input section, object or archive-member name). Numbers are hexadecimal. Discarded COMDATs and debug/directive sections are omitted from contributions. The format is not a JWlink-compatible map: JWlink also reports groups, classes, module-grouped symbols, imports and statistics, and supports additional map controls. No downstream map parser was found in the repository; the build requests a map but does not consume it.

### JWlink directive syntax and option matrix

The repository's real build (`test\run.bat`) invokes JWlink with Watcom-style directives: `JWlink format win pe option map=<file> file a.obj, b.obj lib x.lib y.lib name out.exe`. JWlink2 accepts that form when the first argument is a directive (abbreviations such as `form`, `fi`, `op`, `na` work; `@response-file` may contain directives), translates it to its switch model, and rejects everything else explicitly. Directives and `/switches` cannot be mixed in one command. `JWlink2Test` (`OptionMatrixAcceptsAndRejectsDocumentedForms`, `JwlinkDirectiveSyntaxLinksLikeEquivalentSwitches`) exercises every row below.

| JWlink directive | Status | JWlink2 behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `format windows pe [dll\|exe]` (also `win`, `nt`) | Accepted | `dll` selects `/DLL`; other formats (OS/2, DOS, ELF, bare `format windows`) are rejected |
| `file a,b` | Accepted | Object inputs; `.obj` is appended when no extension is given |
| `library x` / `libfile x` | Accepted | Archive inputs; `.lib` is appended when no extension is given |
| `libpath p;q` | Accepted | Search paths; later `libpath` directives take priority over earlier ones |
| `name out` | Accepted | `/OUT` (`.exe`/`.dll` appended; defaults to the first `file` stem) |
| `system nt \| nt_con \| nt_win \| nt_dll` | Accepted | Console or Windows subsystem; `nt_dll` also selects a DLL |
| `runtime console\|windows[=major.minor]` | Accepted | `/SUBSYSTEM` |
| `debug all \| codeview` | Accepted extension, not JWlink-compatible | `/DEBUG` emits RSDS/PDB; JWlink `debug all` selects DWARF if no format was selected, and `debug codeview` emits NB05 for the tested fixture. `lines`, `types`, `locals`, DWARF, Watcom and Novell output are unsupported |
| `export name[.ordinal][=internal]` | Accepted (bounded) | Creates a named export with an optional ordinal and internal symbol; export keywords and the second `=importname` are unsupported |
| `anonymousexport name.ordinal[=internal]` | Accepted (bounded) | Creates an ordinal-only export; an ordinal is required |
| `reference symbol[,symbol...]` | Accepted | Forces symbol resolution and archive extraction |
| `disable <n>` | Accepted, no effect | JWlink2 emits no numbered warnings |
| `option map[=file]` | Accepted | `/MAP` |
| `option quiet` (`q`) | Accepted | `/NOLOGO` |
| `option start=sym` | Accepted | `/ENTRY` |
| `option nodefaultlibs` | Accepted | `/NODEFAULTLIB` |
| `option caseexact` | Accepted, no effect | Symbol lookup is already case-sensitive |
| `option offset=n` | Accepted | `/BASE` |
| `option objalign=n` | Accepted | `/ALIGN` (section alignment) |
| `option norelocs` | Accepted | `/FIXED` |
| `option implib[=file]` | Accepted | `/IMPLIB` (requires a DLL) |
| `option stack=n,heapsize=n` | Accepted (bounded) | 32-bit reserve sizes; default commit is the smaller of 4096 and reserve. Zero heap size is rejected; `commit` directive is not implemented |
| `option version=major[.minor],osversion=major[.minor],linkversion=major[.minor]` | Accepted (bounded) | Writes PE image, OS and linker versions; minor is at most 99 for image/OS and 255 for linker. Revision components and JWlink warning-and-continue cases are unsupported |
| `option largeaddressaware,nolargeaddressaware` | Accepted | AMD64 is large-address-aware by default; `nolargeaddressaware` clears the bit even if `largeaddressaware` also appears |
| `option nxcompat` | Accepted (JWlink AMD64 behavior) | Clears NX_COMPAT to match the tested JWlink PE32+ output: JWlink sets the flag on its PE32 header but does not copy it to PE32+. Switch-mode `/NXCOMPAT` retains its existing behavior |
| `alias`, `import`, `order`, `segment`, `sort`, `path`, `optlib`, `commit`, `language`, `modfile`, `modtrace`, `symtrace`; other `option`s (`alignment`, `stub`, `nostub`, `checksum`, `undefsok`, `redefsok`, `eliminate`, ...) | Unsupported | Diagnosed with `unsupported JWlink directive/OPTION` |

Source inventory: JWlink `import` parses an internal name and module, optionally followed by `.external-name` or `.ordinal`; `export` parses `name[.ordinal][=internal[=importname]]` plus attributes, and `anonymousexport` uses the same parser with the anonymous flag. `alias` creates a symbol alias, `reference` marks a symbol referenced and retained, `optlib` marks a library optional, `path` is attached to subsequent file entries, and `order`/`segment` have their own grammars. `alignment` rounds file alignment up to a power of two, unlike `objalign`; `stub` names a load file and `nostub` sets a separate flag. `undefsok`/`noundefsok`, `redefsok`/`noredefsok`, and `eliminate` affect resolution and dead-code elimination. These observations are not claims of support for the unsupported forms. The accepted scalar/header forms and precedence are compared against repository JWlink on a small AMD64 object; export and reference behavior is tested in JWlink2, including Windows DLL lookup, but not yet differential-verified. JWlink option abbreviations and separated `=` tokens are not generally implemented.

Switch-style options not listed in the option summary above (`/OPT`, `/LTCG`, `/MACHINE` other than X64 or X86, `/INCREMENTAL` other than `:NO`, and anything else unrecognized) are rejected; the `-l<name>` library shorthand applies only to `-` prefixed arguments.

### Command sources and search paths

Response files use double quotes and Windows-style backslash/quote escaping. They may nest to 32 levels; relative response names and input paths are resolved against the process working directory, not the containing response file. `@name` without an extension searches `name.lnk`; if the file cannot be opened, `name` is looked up as an environment variable containing more command text. A token beginning `%NAME%` expands the variable as command text, with any token suffix appended. Expansion cycles and undefined variables are errors. Response files without a BOM use the process ANSI code page; UTF-8 BOM and UTF-16LE BOM files are decoded to that code page with unrepresentable characters rejected. UTF-16BE and embedded NULs are rejected. This BOM handling is a JWlink2 extension, not a claim about JWlink's encoding support.

For names without a directory, JWlink2 checks the current directory first, then explicit search paths, then environment paths. Directive-style `libpath` entries are searched newest directive first, followed by `LIBPE`, `LIB386`, and `LIB`; switch-style `/LIBPATH` entries are searched in command-line order, followed by `LIB`. Semicolon-separated entries are searched left to right. A name with a directory bypasses search paths. `/ENTRY` and `/ALIGN` on the command line override corresponding COFF directives, while repeated scalar switches such as `/OUT` and `/MAP` use the last occurrence. The tests compare environment command expansion with JWlink and exercise the other rules with controlled subprocess environments.

Compatibility is not yet complete: the directive translator splits commas even inside quoted filenames, percent expansion is supported only at the beginning of a token, and JWlink command-file comments, single-quote syntax, initialization files, full source-location-aware precedence, and object-versus-library search distinctions are not implemented. JWlink2 applies the same search paths to unqualified object names. Additional differential tests against JWlink are needed before claiming full parity.

Additional controls (MSVC-style switches; not available as JWlink directives):

| Switch | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `/INCLUDE:<symbol>` | Forces the symbol to be resolved, pulling in the defining archive member. |
| `/WHOLEARCHIVE[:<lib>]` | The named archive is an explicit input even when not separately listed; its object members are linked wholesale, or all archives' object members are linked wholesale for the bare form. Named archives use library search, append `.lib` when no extension is given, and are deduplicated by case-insensitive resolved lexical path. Short import-object members remain demand-driven. A standalone MSVC `lib.exe` archive is compared and executed with Microsoft LINK and JWlink2; further ordering and import-library behavior are not established. |
| `/STACK:<reserve>[,<commit>]`, `/HEAP:<reserve>[,<commit>]` | Written to the PE optional header (defaults 0x100000 / 0x1000); commit may not exceed reserve. |
| `/MERGE:<from>=<to>` | Merges an output section into another. Linker-managed sections (`.pdata`, `.reloc`, `.idata`, `.edata`, `.rsrc`, `.tls`, `.didat`, `.debug`), self-merges, and chained merges are rejected. |
| `/SECTION:<name>,[ERWSDKP]` | Replaces the memory attribute flags of the named output section. |
| `/MANIFEST[:NO\|:EXTERNAL\|:EMBED[,ID=n]]` | `/MANIFEST` writes `<out>.manifest` (or the `/MANIFESTFILE` path); AMD64 `:EMBED` embeds an `RT_MANIFEST` resource (ID 1 for EXE, 2 for DLL, language 0x409). `/MANIFESTINPUT:<file>` supplies the content and implies embedding; otherwise an `asInvoker` manifest is generated. Embedding fails if an input already has a `.rsrc` section (resource trees are not merged); x86 embedding is unsupported. |
| `/VERBOSE` | Prints each loaded input, the final section layout, and the output path to stdout. |
| `/DEBUG`, `/PDB:<file>` | See the debug-information section. |

`.res` inputs are still not implemented; resources supplied as COFF `.rsrc` sections (for example from `cvtres`) pass through to the PE resource directory. Windows resource APIs are tested against generated EXE and DLL manifests by type, ID and language; this does not validate application activation or resource-tree merging.

